\description{
Single entry point that fits one propensity score and hands it to any
combination of MatchIt's matching algorithms, then reports how much sample
each one keeps, how its matching weights are distributed and how well it
balances the covariates -- so that nearest-neighbour, optimal, full and
coarsened exact matching can be compared on one table rather than one at a
time.
}
\details{
\code{get_PSM()} constructs and diagnoses matched cohorts; it does not estimate
a treatment effect. Feed \code{result$data} with the weight column of your
choice to an outcome model, clustering on the matching subclass, as in
\code{lm(y ~ z, data = res$data, weights = w_nearest)} with
\code{sandwich::vcovCL(cluster = res$data$s_nearest)}.
}
\section{Relation to get_PSW()}{

The two functions are duals and share their score, their \code{w_} column
convention and their diagnostics table. One name differs: in \code{\link[=get_PSW]{get_PSW()}}
\code{method} selects the \strong{propensity model}, while here \code{method} selects the
\strong{matching algorithm} -- MatchIt's own meaning -- and the propensity model
is \code{ps_method}. Matching weights are themselves inverse-probability
weights, with the modelled score replaced by the treated share inside each
matched set, so \code{w_nearest} and \code{\link[=get_PSW]{get_PSW()}}'s \code{w_att} target the same
estimand by different routes.
}

\section{Which estimand each method allows}{

Measured against MatchIt 4.7.2, not assumed. \code{"nearest"}, \code{"optimal"} and
\code{"genetic"} accept \code{"ATT"} and \code{"ATC"} only; \code{"full"}, \code{"cem"}, \code{"exact"}
and \code{"subclass"} accept \code{"ATE"} as well. Asking for an unsupported pair is
an error naming the offending method, rather than MatchIt's own message,
which does not say that another method would work.

\code{"optimal"} and \code{"full"} need \pkg{optmatch}; \code{"genetic"} needs
\pkg{rgenoud} and is slow. \code{"cem"} is built into MatchIt and needs nothing.

Not every method takes every knob, and MatchIt warns once per ignored
argument, so each method is handed only what it uses: \code{ratio} reaches
\code{"nearest"}, \code{"optimal"} and \code{"genetic"}; \code{caliper} reaches \code{"nearest"},
\code{"full"} and \code{"genetic"}; \code{replace} reaches \code{"nearest"} and \code{"genetic"}.
\code{"cem"} and \code{"exact"} ignore the propensity score altogether -- they
coarsen or match on the covariates themselves -- so they are compared
alongside the others rather than sharing their score. What each method
actually received is recorded in \code{attr(x, "analysis")$specs}.
}

\section{Weight normalisation}{

MatchIt's \code{normalize = TRUE} default rescales each arm to a mean of 1,
which removes the inverse-probability reading of the weights: at
\code{ratio = 2} the control weights would sum to twice the number of treated
units. \code{get_PSM()} fixes \code{normalize = FALSE}, so an ATT control column sums
to the number of treated units, as the textbook weight does. The effective
sample size is scale-free and is the same under either setting.
}

